@page "/docs/cli/blocks"

@* izzy add <block>: where a block goes, --into and --at, recipes, settings, and the .Client project. *@

<ArticlePage Section="cli" Slug="blocks">
    <Lead>
        A block is a section of a page, such as a hero, pricing or a sign-up form. <Code>izzy add</Code> writes its
        component into your app and places it on a page. Every block is on <Anchor Href="blocks">Blocks</Anchor>, with
        its settings beside a live preview.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Add a block</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Run <Code>izzy add</Code> in the app's folder, with the block's settings as options:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy add hero --layout Split</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            The block's component goes in <Code>Components/Blocks</Code>, and the block goes on its page: a section at the
            end of <Code>Home.razor</Code>, a footer in the layout.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Place it</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>--into</Code> names another page, and <Code>--at</Code> puts it on a line, as an editor's cursor does. A
            recipe, such as an empty state, goes only where you put it:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy add empty-state --at Components/Pages/Orders.razor:24</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">Settings</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>izzy describe hero</Code> lists a block's settings. They have the same names as the starter's options in
            <Code>dotnet new</Code>, so one spelling works in both.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Apps whose pages run in the browser</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            In an app with WebAssembly or Auto pages, blocks go in the <Code>.Client</Code> project with the pages. The
            parts that read the request, such as the cookie notice's, stay in the server project.
        </TextBlock>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>
